swer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673308} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067b47fe-5b13-5372-b6e1-c325e897caf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673282} {:answer/id #uuid "4acceec2-cd60-5eef-a392-4bcdd647f18d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673292}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "067b47fe-5b13-5372-b6e1-c325e897caf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673282} :answer-field/id #uuid "a491c228-bac9-5413-a905-66f5521c9aed" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673313}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b1f3c08b-d01d-52cf-889e-bfab6bc22038" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' + \\frac{3 \\cdot y^{3}}{x^{2}} = 2 \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y' - 2 \\cdot y \\cdot e^{x} = 5 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y^{5}$\n- **III.** $y' + 5 \\cdot y^{4} \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = - 3 \\cdot x \\cdot y^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=- 2 \\cdot e^{x},\\quad q(x)=5 \\cdot x^{2},\\quad n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\nTherefore, select II."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186673324 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63425b5c-327c-5bff-8c43-f4ed8cc6fafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673301} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ee5673-0a5d-5123-830b-50503df0d6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7ee5673-0a5d-5123-830b-50503df0d6bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673325} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bea2f66-1e31-5fef-88db-6a6425f152d8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673296} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b4a7d7d-266d-5a97-afe0-d74dcaf50695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673293} {:answer/id #uuid "de4a701c-ff88-5cb5-9c37-6ad527236f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de4a701c-ff88-5cb5-9c37-6ad527236f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673322} :answer-field/id #uuid "89113077-dd44-5d5c-93df-87bb21b128c3" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673309} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6a9886-d056-5f56-b96d-110a66150149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673283} {:answer/id #uuid "aa20897a-ed31-593d-be0d-e229b82e4d48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a6a9886-d056-5f56-b96d-110a66150149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673283} :answer-field/id #uuid "902c375f-f748-5a4d-a548-dbdefc54c9c1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673310} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57c37124-1500-5755-9d3e-350274e41638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673295} {:answer/id #uuid "f95af1e6-c0e2-5d2d-8bb1-e4550e4c0f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57c37124-1500-5755-9d3e-350274e41638" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673295} :answer-field/id #uuid "f28f64b6-0945-576e-993a-ff64ebcb50a6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186673328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e6954ca4-0576-566d-ba72-0d26e66452ab" :question/problem "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\n- **I.** $y' + \\frac{3 \\cdot y^{3}}{x^{2}} = 5 \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$\n- **II.** $y' - y \\cdot e^{x} = 5 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y^{5}$\n- **III.** $y' + 2 \\cdot y \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = - 2 \\cdot x \\cdot y^{3}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation I is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation II is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=- e^{x},\\quad q(x)=5 \\cdot x^{2},\\quad n=5.\n$$\n\n- Equation III is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=2 \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)},\\quad q(x)=- 2 \\cdot x,\\quad n=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, select II, III."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186689492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b813de-245f-5b27-a680-6053e166ec52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{(6x+C)^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689486} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3b92ec-8632-5954-a8b2-8115d08aff5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{(3\\sqrt x+C)^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689489} {:answer/id #uuid "9b58a8e0-d828-5ca8-85a7-9aaaaafeba77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\left(\\frac{6\\sqrt x+C}{x^3}\\right)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689500} {:answer/id #uuid "b88105d7-5c02-54f6-8cf7-4ca33f2825bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{(6\\sqrt x+C)^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689501} {:answer/id #uuid "e8318eac-4d31-5369-aeac-05c6e2980373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\left(\\frac{6\\sqrt x+C}{x^{3/2}}\\right)^{3/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b88105d7-5c02-54f6-8cf7-4ca33f2825bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{(6\\sqrt x+C)^{2/3}}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689501} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cf9fe93-d941-5cd9-a6ac-fd3e5f2a1282" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3e118a37-a28f-5ed8-b64f-3e20a903490d" :question/problem "Find the general positive real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt y}-xy,\\qquad x>0.\n$$\n\nIn each option, $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Work on intervals where the bases of fractional powers in your solution are positive." :question/worked-solution "First rearrange and divide by $x^2$ to expose the Bernoulli form:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y}{x}=\\frac{2}{x^2}y^{-1/2}.\n$$\n\nHere $p(x)=1/x$, $q(x)=2/x^2$, and $n=-\\tfrac12$. Multiply by $\\sqrt y$:\n\n$$\ny^{1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+\\frac{y^{3/2}}{x}=\\frac{2}{x^2}.\n$$\n\nSet $z=y^{1-n}=y^{3/2}>0$. The chain rule gives $z'=\\tfrac32 y^{1/2}y'$, hence\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}z'+\\frac{z}{x}=\\frac{2}{x^2},\\qquad z'+\\frac{3}{2x}z=\\frac{3}{x^2}.\n$$\n\nSince $x>0$, an integrating factor is\n\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int 3/(2x)\\,dx}=e^{(3/2)\\ln x}=x^{3/2}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying by this factor gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}(x^{3/2}z)=3x^{-1/2}.\n$$\n\nIntegrate and solve for $z$:\n\n$$\nx^{3/2}z=6\\sqrt x+C,\\qquad z=\\frac{6\\sqrt x+C}{x^{3/2}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $z=y^{3/2}$, so raise the expression for $z$ to the power $2/3$:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(6\\sqrt x+C)^{2/3}}{x}.\n$$\n\nUse only intervals with $x>0$ and $6\\sqrt x+C>0$, since $z=y^{3/2}$ must be positive. The equation is undefined at $y=0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186689494 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db94f2d-9e50-5fc8-acd5-70610f90da93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(Ce^x-1)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689488} {:answer/id #uuid "24a36d3e-ce82-5441-a58a-287d36f96f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=Ce^x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689490} {:answer/id #uuid "75f8fd53-ae2c-59c5-84e8-0eba5245d9ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{Ce^x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689495} {:answer/id #uuid "7f97b84e-27a4-5a77-8a1e-2bc375c5443d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(Ce^x+1)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689496} {:answer/id #uuid "8d2bcc3a-1d08-5050-9568-df1bdcbbfa00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(Ce^{2x}-1)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1db94f2d-9e50-5fc8-acd5-70610f90da93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(Ce^x-1)^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689488} :answer-field/id #uuid "9894f867-d86b-56af-82b7-8be07d03d6e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689499}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6bf4d16d-0efe-5a8e-adb2-a67ca4f82c9b" :question/problem "Find the general positive real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}-2y=2\\sqrt{y}.\n$$\n\nIn each option, $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Work on intervals where the chosen expression for $\\sqrt y$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with $p(x)=-2$, $q(x)=2$, and $n=\\tfrac12$. Since $y>0$, divide by $\\sqrt y$:\n\n$$\ny^{-1/2}\\frac{dy}{dx}-2y^{1/2}=2.\n$$\n\nSet $z=y^{1-n}=\\sqrt y>0$. The chain rule gives $z'=\\tfrac12 y^{-1/2}y'$, so\n\n$$\n2z'-2z=2,\\qquad z'-z=1.\n$$\n\nAn integrating factor is $I(x)=e^{-x}$. Thus\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}(e^{-x}z)=e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating gives\n\n$$\ne^{-x}z=-e^{-x}+C,\\qquad z=Ce^x-1.\n$$\n\nSubstitute back and square:\n\n$$\ny=(Ce^x-1)^2.\n$$\n\nRetain only intervals where $Ce^x-1>0$, because this expression equals $\\sqrt y$. Squaring a negative expression would introduce functions that do not satisfy the original equation. The zero solution is outside the requested positive family."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186689506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b83af12-237e-579f-9951-efda4b965087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+Ce^x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689487} {:answer/id #uuid "2e64edc7-57d9-5517-9dc9-82db72f4f279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+Ce^{-x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689491} {:answer/id #uuid "89900cf9-1f73-5f98-a27c-1e73a0cb5023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{Ce^x-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689497} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5d174a-41f4-5a25-b92a-b04fff004ec5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=1+Ce^x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689504} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f95713-f0f0-5605-b0b6-19ba7ec74ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+Ce^{2x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689505}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b83af12-237e-579f-9951-efda4b965087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+Ce^x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689487} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdd85d6b-f295-5cdf-9651-81d3d1f3e151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689502}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fb29bfc8-1f67-5c3e-9b7f-8314159f8153" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+y=y^2.\n$$\n\nUse the substitution $z=y^{-1}$. In each option, $C$ is an arbitrary constant. Work on an interval where the solution is defined." :question/worked-solution "Here $p(x)=1$, $q(x)=1$, and $n=2$. For a nonzero solution, divide by $y^2$:\n\n$$\ny^{-2}\\frac{dy}{dx}+y^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nWith $z=y^{-1}$, the chain rule gives $z'=-y^{-2}y'$. Hence\n\n$$\n-z'+z=1,\\qquad z'-z=-1.\n$$\n\nThe integrating factor is $I(x)=e^{\\int -1\\,dx}=e^{-x}$. Multiplying by it gives a product derivative:\n\n$$\n\\frac{d}{dx}(e^{-x}z)=-e^{-x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating and then dividing by $e^{-x}$ gives\n\n$$\ne^{-x}z=e^{-x}+C,\\qquad z=1+Ce^x.\n$$\n\nFinally, $z=1/y$, so\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{1+Ce^x}.\n$$\n\nExclude any point where $1+Ce^x=0$. The original equation also admits $y=0$, which was excluded by the question's nonzero condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186689701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aeeeffeb-72f0-5b19-94aa-1ced3daef26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\left(\\frac{C}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{9}{2}}} + \\frac{9}{7 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)}\\right)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689696} {:answer/id #uuid "daff41fe-effb-5812-a242-669fd85f61f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\left(\\frac{C}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} - \\frac{3}{\\left(x+5\\right)}\\right)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689698} {:answer/id #uuid "dc042419-be15-5e7e-aabd-35cd5e276065" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\left(\\frac{C}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} - \\frac{6}{\\left(x+5\\right)}\\right)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689699} {:answer/id #uuid "dc963931-7b85-5e98-9069-dc19a6734a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\left(\\frac{C}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{3}{2}}} + \\frac{9}{\\left(x+5\\right)}\\right)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689700} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c7b55d-a8ee-53a6-ba9f-bbfa1ebf5eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\left(\\frac{C}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{3}} - \\frac{3}{4 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)}\\right)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aeeeffeb-72f0-5b19-94aa-1ced3daef26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\left(\\frac{C}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{9}{2}}} + \\frac{9}{7 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)}\\right)^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689696} :answer-field/id #uuid "b14891dc-eb26-53af-9350-b11a114a6f1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f4f7f94f-2de7-5897-b4a4-eaffaf194a2c" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\left(x+5\\right)^{2}\\,\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- 3 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right) \\cdot y + \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{y}}.\n$$\n\nWork on an interval where $\\left(x+5\\right)>0$. Take $y>0$. For even powers and powers of $2/3$ in the choices, retain only intervals where the base is positive. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite." :question/worked-solution "First, rearrange and divide by the coefficient of the derivative:\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\frac{3 \\cdot y}{\\left(x+5\\right)}=\\frac{3}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{2} \\cdot \\sqrt{y}}. $$\n\nThis is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=\\frac{3}{\\left(x+5\\right)}\\\\ q(x)&=\\frac{3}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}\\\\ n&=- \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{- \\frac{1}{2}}$ and set $z=y^{\\frac{3}{2}}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{3 \\cdot \\sqrt{y}}{2}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(\\frac{9}{2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)}\\right)z=\\frac{9}{2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{2}}.$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int \\frac{9}{2 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{9}{2}}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{9}{2}}\\,z\\right)=\\frac{9 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{5}{2}}}{2}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{9}{2}}z=\\frac{9 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{7}{2}}}{7}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=\\frac{C}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{9}{2}}} + \\frac{9}{7 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)}.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{\\frac{3}{2}}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\left(\\frac{C}{\\left(x+5\\right)^{\\frac{9}{2}}} + \\frac{9}{7 \\cdot \\left(x+5\\right)}\\right)^{2/3}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals. The displayed expression for $z$ must be positive because $z=y^{\\frac{3}{2}}>0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186689708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03aff173-5fe4-508c-a4ac-530618707ba9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{C \\cdot e^{x^{2}} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689704} {:answer/id #uuid "180c15bc-6b27-5c13-8732-5f92549609ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{3 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{\\frac{3 \\cdot x^{2}}{2}} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689706} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4cf932-596e-55b3-a8c5-abf59287a2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{x^{2}} + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689713} {:answer/id #uuid "9734508a-c672-575b-817c-1b49724e1da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{3 \\cdot x^{2}}{2}} - 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689721} {:answer/id #uuid "f561315f-e655-56cc-aea4-a84257612018" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{C \\cdot e^{\\frac{x^{2}}{2}} - 2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a4cf932-596e-55b3-a8c5-abf59287a2c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{x^{2}} + 3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689713} :answer-field/id #uuid "90827aac-03a0-531e-ad56-5ce0aa381723" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2ddefef1-d3a2-54f5-af28-279657ba8e80" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}+2 \\cdot x \\cdot y=3 \\cdot x \\cdot y^{2}.\n$$\n\nUse the substitution $z=y^{-1}$.\n\nWork on an interval where $x>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite." :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=2 \\cdot x\\\\ q(x)&=3 \\cdot x\\\\ n&=2.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{2}$ and set $z=y^{-1}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\frac{1}{y^{2}}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(- 2 \\cdot x\\right)z=- 3 \\cdot x.$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int - 2 \\cdot x\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{- x^{2}}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(e^{- x^{2}}\\,z\\right)=- 3 \\cdot x \\cdot e^{- x^{2}}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$e^{- x^{2}}z=\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{- x^{2}}}{2}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=C \\cdot e^{x^{2}} + \\frac{3}{2}.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{-1}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\frac{2}{2 \\cdot C \\cdot e^{x^{2}} + 3}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186689711 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8384417d-bf46-5bb3-bdaa-274aa69b3f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot x}}{3 \\cdot C + e^{3 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689719} {:answer/id #uuid "b79cb045-ec9f-5c39-9109-b69384d72a64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot x}}{3 \\cdot C - 2 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689726} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfe3a8c-05cd-52a3-bf6d-d3176ef16a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{e^{x}}{C + e^{2 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689727} {:answer/id #uuid "e395addf-26ba-5a5d-873e-9200dfa82d17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot x}}{3 \\cdot C - e^{3 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689728} {:answer/id #uuid "ff6b5c3f-ba95-558a-bbef-00879a4f518e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{e^{2 \\cdot x}}{C + e^{3 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689731}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8384417d-bf46-5bb3-bdaa-274aa69b3f6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot x}}{3 \\cdot C + e^{3 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689719} :answer-field/id #uuid "6844e8ba-c096-5eef-bf00-00f2bc80bd8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4f2eea55-fd5a-5f18-a136-ca5ba21ec0c1" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}- 2 \\cdot y=- y^{2} \\cdot e^{x}.\n$$\n\nUse the substitution $z=y^{-1}$.\n\nWork on an interval where $x>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite." :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=-2\\\\ q(x)&=- e^{x}\\\\ n&=2.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{2}$ and set $z=y^{-1}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\frac{1}{y^{2}}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(2\\right)z=e^{x}.$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int 2\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{2 \\cdot x}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(e^{2 \\cdot x}\\,z\\right)=e^{3 \\cdot x}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$e^{2 \\cdot x}z=\\frac{e^{3 \\cdot x}}{3}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=C \\cdot e^{- 2 \\cdot x} + \\frac{e^{x}}{3}.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{-1}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot x}}{3 \\cdot C + e^{3 \\cdot x}}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186689716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "074ad3ec-03c8-54b8-80e1-f09e25ee9ab9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot x}}{3 \\cdot C - 2 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689705} {:answer/id #uuid "187b35d6-3602-5b03-9d9b-1eca839e228f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}{4 \\cdot C + 3 \\cdot e^{4 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689707} {:answer/id #uuid "5d9918be-1b63-5999-bff6-cf31dcef863c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot x}}{3 \\cdot C + 2 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689715} {:answer/id #uuid "7365ccc9-231a-5a29-97c4-4459ff39b3fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2 \\cdot e^{x}}{2 \\cdot C - 3 \\cdot e^{2 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689718} {:answer/id #uuid "f4713fb1-038a-5cd1-b6d3-37eba5459a3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2 \\cdot e^{x}}{2 \\cdot C - e^{2 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "187b35d6-3602-5b03-9d9b-1eca839e228f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}{4 \\cdot C + 3 \\cdot e^{4 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689707} :answer-field/id #uuid "98062bac-cf1a-532f-b8db-546cba377000" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "643f8f63-f6a3-5fcd-aa4c-2fb509ad42f2" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}- 3 \\cdot y=- 3 \\cdot y^{2} \\cdot e^{x}.\n$$\n\nUse the substitution $z=y^{-1}$.\n\nWork on an interval where $x>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite." :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=-3\\\\ q(x)&=- 3 \\cdot e^{x}\\\\ n&=2.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{2}$ and set $z=y^{-1}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\frac{1}{y^{2}}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(3\\right)z=3 \\cdot e^{x}.$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int 3\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{3 \\cdot x}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(e^{3 \\cdot x}\\,z\\right)=3 \\cdot e^{4 \\cdot x}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$e^{3 \\cdot x}z=\\frac{3 \\cdot e^{4 \\cdot x}}{4}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=C \\cdot e^{- 3 \\cdot x} + \\frac{3 \\cdot e^{x}}{4}.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{-1}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\frac{4 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}{4 \\cdot C + 3 \\cdot e^{4 \\cdot x}}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186689725 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e7ec805-40e3-5e05-a5da-ca1fcd3abf44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{4 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}{4 \\cdot C + 3 \\cdot e^{4 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689709} {:answer/id #uuid "5a10df62-f22a-563c-88b6-08c041d371d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}{2 \\cdot C - e^{4 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689712} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba0bcff-b26b-5a42-8d27-78d38826f4ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{e^{2 \\cdot x}}{C + e^{3 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689714} {:answer/id #uuid "a85c0eed-081a-5d7c-8e37-6f88e92cd914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2 \\cdot e^{x}}{2 \\cdot C - e^{2 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689723} {:answer/id #uuid "a9e19d31-1121-53d6-b530-c79e66aa5547" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}{2 \\cdot C + e^{4 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a10df62-f22a-563c-88b6-08c041d371d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}{2 \\cdot C - e^{4 \\cdot x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689712} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fcefe3b-b30c-591a-b4d6-28fc02a44696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186689710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ad81e65e-586a-5671-8f52-c40bea7f2b7f" :question/problem "Find the general nonzero real solution to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}- 3 \\cdot y=2 \\cdot y^{2} \\cdot e^{x}.\n$$\n\nUse the substitution $z=y^{-1}$.\n\nWork on an interval where $x>0$. Choose constants and intervals on which the displayed expressions are real and finite." :question/worked-solution "This is a Bernoulli equation with\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}p(x)&=-3\\\\ q(x)&=2 \\cdot e^{x}\\\\ n&=2.\\end{aligned}$$\n\nDivide by $y^{2}$ and set $z=y^{-1}$. The chain rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}=- \\frac{1}{y^{2}}\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$$\n\nHence the linear equation in standard form is\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}z}{\\mathrm{d}x}+\\left(3\\right)z=- 2 \\cdot e^{x}.$$\n\nAn integrating factor is\n\n$$I(x)=e^{\\int 3\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{3 \\cdot x}.$$\n\nMultiplying through and using the product rule gives\n\n$$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}\\left(e^{3 \\cdot x}\\,z\\right)=- 2 \\cdot e^{4 \\cdot x}.$$\n\nIntegrating with respect to $x$, we obtain\n\n$$e^{3 \\cdot x}z=- \\frac{e^{4 \\cdot x}}{2}+C.$$\n\nThus\n\n$$z=C \\cdot e^{- 3 \\cdot x} - \\frac{e^{x}}{2}.$$\n\nFinally, substitute $z=y^{-1}$ and solve for $y$:\n\n$$y=\\frac{2 \\cdot e^{3 \\cdot x}}{2 \\cdot C - e^{4 \\cdot x}}.$$\n\nHere $C$ is an arbitrary real constant; exclude singularities and restrict to the stated real intervals."}]}
